<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matilda of England, Duchess of Saxony</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1156">1156</a>–<a href="/years/1189">1189</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-plantagenet">House of Plantagenet</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matilda of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> was the eldest daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a>. In 1168 she married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony">Duke of Saxony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, and lived at his court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, where she was a patron of literature. After Henry was deposed in 1180 she accompanied him into exile at her father's court. Their son Otto IV became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matilda-of-england-duchess-of-saxony.jpg" alt="Matilda of England, Duchess of Saxony" width="320" height="796" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Helmarshausen Benedictine monastery · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matildaofengland.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Matilda was born in or around June 1156, probably in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, the third child and eldest daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a>, and was named after her paternal grandmother, Empress Matilda. In 1165 the Holy Roman Emperor Frederick I sent an embassy under Rainald of Dassel, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, to arrange a double marriage of Henry II's daughters Matilda and Eleanor to the emperor's son Frederick V of Swabia and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony">Duke of Saxony</a>. Only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> match was agreed.</p><p>Matilda returned from Normandy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in the autumn of 1166, and in 1167 she travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> with her mother. She married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> in Minden Cathedral on 1 February 1168. He was 27 years older than she and had divorced his first wife, Clementia of Zähringen, in 1162.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The duke's residence was at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>. There Matilda bore her first child, Richenza, in 1172 while her husband was on pilgrimage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>, and James Panton writes that in the early years she ruled his estates in his absence. The couple held a large court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dankwarderode-castle">Dankwarderode Castle</a>, had Brunswick Cathedral built from 1173, and initiated the Lucidarius, the first original German prose work, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>, a masterpiece of Romanesque illumination.</p><p>In 1180 Henry was tried in absentia at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> and stripped of his lands, after refusing in 1174 to join Frederick on an expedition against Normandy, which belonged to Matilda's father. When the emperor besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, where Matilda had just given birth to her son Lothair, she addressed him as a knight, but he sent only a keg of wine.</p><p>Henry obeyed in November 1181 and went into exile, and Matilda chose to join him at her father's court, with all the children except Lothair, who remained a hostage. In Normandy she met the troubadour Bertran de Born, who addressed two poems of courtly love to her. In 1185, at Henry II's request, the family was restored to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In spring 1189 the emperor ordered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> or go into exile, and he went again to his father-in-law, while Matilda stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> with her children to defend his interests. She died there in June or July 1189, sources giving various days, and was buried in Brunswick Cathedral. Henry, who died in 1195, wished to lie at her right hand. Five children are certainly documented, among them Henry, who made peace between the Welf and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> houses by his marriage, Otto, who became Otto IV, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>, and William, from whom the dukes of Brunswick and Luneburg descend.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Matilda of England, Duchess of Saxony” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Windsor Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Brunswick, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.48,-0.6&amp;year=1177" aria-label="Windsor Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4536 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Matilda of England, Duchess of Saxony"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4986 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4986 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.48,-0.6&amp;year=1177">What was here in 1177? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
